    <decisions>
        <description>Roll back uncommitted records from this transaction before showing the error, unless the caller opted out.</description>
        <name>Roll_Back_Decision</name>
        <label>Roll back records?</label>
        <locationX>176</locationX>
        <locationY>242</locationY>
        <defaultConnector>
            <targetReference>Fault_Screen</targetReference>
        </defaultConnector>
        <defaultConnectorLabel>No</defaultConnectorLabel>
        <rules>
            <name>Yes_Roll_Back</name>
            <conditionLogic>and</conditionLogic>
            <conditions>
                <leftValueReference>rollBackRecords</leftValueReference>
                <operator>EqualTo</operator>
                <rightValue>
                    <booleanValue>true</booleanValue>
                </rightValue>
            </conditions>
            <connector>
                <targetReference>Roll_Back_Records</targetReference>
            </connector>
            <label>Yes</label>
        </rules>
    </decisions>
    <description>Reusable fault handler for screen flows. Call from any fault connector: pass $Flow.FaultMessage, your flow's API name and the faulting element's name. It classifies and logs the fault (via FlowFaultHandlerCore), rolls back uncommitted records in the current transaction (optional), shows the standard error screen with Try again / Cancel, and returns selectedAction so the caller can loop back or end. Logging survives the roll back because Nebula Logger publishes platform events immediately.</description>
